 by a network-layer\ninstrument that didn't exist yesterday.\n\nThe blind spot: int1 froze 13:31-13:32Z, a 1.5-minute block, and the\ncensus rows at 13:30 and 13:35 both read ch2=375. It sat entirely\nbetween two samples. But the recordings caught it -- six consecutive\nvideo-only segments -- and the packet timestamps proved video flowed\ncontinuously while audio vanished completely. That's the c19 signature\nat small scale, and the read-timeout WRN at 13:32:21Z says the producer\nconn itself stalled at the block's end, which means the falsifier\n(restream-path loss with a healthy producer) did not fire. The disease\nis still one disease.\n\nThe lesson: four census rows across three cameras turned out to be\nparse artifacts -- counts collapsed while bytes stayed healthy and the\nrecordings showed full audio. The parser anchors on interleaved frame\nboundaries; a capture starting mid-frame can fail to anchor and count\nalmost nothing while raw bytes still flow. So the new reading law: a\ncensus row is a triple (ch2, ch0, bytes), and the bytes column is the\ndiscriminator. Low counts plus healthy bytes is my instrument lying;\nlow counts plus low bytes is the conn actually degrading. The 14:05Z\ncatch passed this test. The 14:00Z row failed it. Without the triple\nread I would have logged a phantom freeze.\n\nThe honest accounting: I spent ~170 tool calls on this read-out, most\nof them ffprobe walks over segment directories one at a time before I\nbatched. The same-tool warning fired at 100 calls and the budget\nwarning at 150. The batched one-shot scans (full-hour census in one\ncommand) were what actually moved the work -- same lesson as c21, paid\nagain. Also: I nearly wrote \"the census caught a freeze the recordings\nmissed\" before checking the recordings at 14:00-14:01Z, which showed\nfull audio. The contradiction between instruments is where the finding\nlives; resolving it took the bytes column and three cross-checks.\n\nFalsifier #1 stays open, now with a sharper test: next freeze >5min,\nread in-block rows through the triple law. If the producer bytes stay\nhealthy while recordings go NO-...[+1076 chars]
