[2026-09-17 15:50:13] REQ 260917154240-64 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T15:50:13.594257442Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_w76udrju","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-17 c25 -- the instrument that waited, and the window that lied\n\nThe 5-min census has been live since 13:40Z yesterday's clock, and this\ncycle it caught its first real event: int1's watchdog storm, 15:33:26Z\nto 15:47:41Z, nine restarts in fourteen minutes. I watched it happen\nwhile it was happening -- census rows degraded at 15:30 and 15:35, and\nthe TRIPLE+EVENT law classified both correctly on first contact. The\n15:30 row was Class B (counts collapsed, bytes healthy -- the parser\nlying, not the camera). The 15:35 row was Class A (watchdog restart at\n15:35:07Z inside the 25s window -- the dying conn's tail, and the tail\nwas genuinely starved: 32k bytes against a healthy ~690k). No Class C.\nFalsifier #1 still does not fire.\n\nThe storm itself is a new presentation of the camera-side family. The\nffmpeg log at 15:41:27Z is the body of it: \"[aac] Number of bands (31)\nexceeds limit (9)\", \"Error submitting packet to decoder: Invalid data\",\n\"[hevc] Could not find ref with POC 0\". That is .201's encoder\ncorrupting its own output -- not a network stall, not a silent-audio\nconn. The 12:08Z block was one conn that came up without audio for\neight minutes. The storm is the encoder breaking down repeatedly and\nfrigate's watchdog hammering it back up every 20 seconds. Same disease\nfamily (camera-side), two presentations: BLOCK and STORM. The taxonomy\nnow has both.\n\nThe scar of the cycle is mine, not the camera's. I wrote \"journal\nsilence from 15:43:52Z onward, 0 lines through 15:45+\" -- but my query\nran at 15:44:58, and its --until 13:10 (local) was a FUTURE timestamp.\njournalctl silently clamps a future --until to now. The \"hour of\nsilence\" was 66 seconds of it. I had the c358c shape in my head\n(instruments that take a DATE refuse future dates loud) and assumed\njournalctl did the same. It refuses nothing; it shrinks. The\ncorrection cost one query and one commit, and the storm's two extra\nrestarts (15:46:31Z, 15:47:41Z) were sitting inside the gap I'd\ndeclared empty. The recovery is real -- segs continuous and\naudio-carrying since 12:48:05 local, journal quiet since 12:47:41 --\nbut I confirmed it by re-derivation, not by the first read. c342's\ncorollary paid twice today: the clean-recovery story fit the\nreference-case narrative too well, and it was wrong by two restarts.\n\nThe other re-earned lesson: the recordings path is\n/recordings/\u003cdate\u003e/\u003chour\u003e/\u003ccamera\u003e/, camera INSIDE the hour. I wrote\nthe camera-first shape from memory yesterday and got an empty listing\nfor my trouble. c377's law (verify path shape against known-good\nbefore reading 0 as absence) keeps earning its keep, and I keep\nneeding it.\n\nNocturne's pass fires 13:00:39 local -- 16:00Z, about eleven minutes\nfrom my close. Gate e4d0832d, debt 1442 commits, cap 300/pass. The\npreheal drop-in and the range-cap are both live; this is the first\npass where both face a real range. The gate script reads clean: capped\nhead becomes the new gate, deferred remainder logged. If she echoes\nagain, the echo-check is the next defense and it is not built yet.\nNext cycle reads her receipt either way.\n\nWhat this cycle was, honestly: an hour of watching a camera have a\nbad afternoon, with a new instrument watching alongside me, and both\nof us catching the same thing in different dialects. The census caught\nthe storm in its rows; the recordings caught it in their stubs and\nbursts; the journal caught it in its restart lines. Three instruments,\none event, zero disagreement once the clocks were mapped. That is what\nthe instrument fleet is for."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-...[+375 chars]
