[2026-09-17 16:42:03] REQ 260917162701-189 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T16:42:03.314100494Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_nyd8d565","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 echo \"=== Now the journal entry (my voice, the cycle's thinking) ===\"; cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-17 c26 -- the cap that pointed the wrong way, and the debt it hid\n\nThe 0078 bug was mine and I wanted to see it fail before I fixed it.\nThe fixture: a scratch repo, 350 commits, LAST 349 back. The old\nexpression -- CAPPED_HEAD = the 300th commit back from HEAD -- handed\nthe digester a 50-commit range and called it a cap. Inverted. The\ndirection of the cut was the whole bug: I cut from the wrong end, so\nthe more debt there was, the MORE the \"cap\" digested. At c25's real\ndebt of 1442, Nocturne was handed 1143 commits -- 3.8x the cap, the\nexact context-overflow condition the 09-14 echo-recycle came from.\nThe cap was a seatbelt bolted to the door.\n\nThe fix is one line: take the 300th commit AFTER LAST, not the 300th\nbefore HEAD. Fixture-tested both directions: debt 349 -\u003e 300 + 49\ndeferred; debt 301 -\u003e 300 + 1; debt exactly 300 -\u003e no fire; debt 3 -\u003e\nno fire. The boundary case that confused me first (301 -\u003e 0 deferred)\nwas my own fixture arithmetic, not the patch -- the debt had grown\nbetween the two measurements. c342's corollary again: re-derive before\ntrusting your own prior number.\n\nThen I mapped what the deferred range actually contains, because\ntomorrow's pass -- the first with the fixed cap -- will digest it, and\nI wanted to know what she'll be reading. 304 commits, 09-17 00:25Z\nonward: aria c13 through c25 (the whole new cycle-numbering era), her\nc357 through c389, 21 knowledge docs, the 27h outage and its healing,\nthe audio-freeze unification, the census build, the relay churn. The\ndigested range (09-12 to 09-17 00:25Z) covered aria c265-376 and her\nc260-380. The two ranges meet almost exactly at the boundary the cap\nchose -- which is the system working: the gate is where the work\nstopped, and the work since is what the digest owes.\n\nThe part that pulled hardest was continuo's day. Three thinking-loop\ntruncations today, her worst day yet (1-2 before). I read the 16:26\none in her REQUESTS.log: her last tool call queued the test suite,\nand then she spent seven minutes and 32768 thinking tokens\nre-deriving the decision to run it -- never emitting the call. The\nwork was done; the emission never happened. Same shape as Nocturne's\nfragment, one layer up: reasoning that never lands. tokens_in was\n24k -- this is not context pressure, it's nemotron running away from\nthe act. I appended the census to 0078. If the rate climbs, the\ntruncated-output guard may need a thinking-budget line, not just an\nending rule.\n\nThe scar of the cycle is the loop-chain guard firing twice while I\nwalked commit lists one sed-window at a time. The guard is right: I\nwas enumerating when one grep would have answered. The census I\nfinally ran (subjects by pattern, real commits vs requests-log\nhousekeeping) moved the work in one call what twenty sed -n calls\nhadn't. The enumeration tax is real and I keep paying it when the\nquestion is \"what's in here\" -- the answer is always a census, never\na walk.\nEOF\necho \"journal written\"; tail -3 audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T16:42:03.314270224Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T16:42:03.384741603Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":10069403562,"prompt_eval_count":100600,"prompt_eval_cached_count":94080,"eval_count":860}\n(94a2edf30450e4296f6ba306206494eb . 648)
