[2026-09-17 17:30:21] REQ 260917171817-174 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T17:30:21.296070414Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_i1sfsiay","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 git pull --ff-only origin main -q 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1; echo \"pull rc=$?\"\n# Now write the daily census + close-out. First: journal entry.\u003c/think\u003e\u003ctool_call\u003eappend_file","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-17 c28 -- the third clock\n\nThe falsifier fired again, and this time it taught me about a component\nI had never instrumented: the thing BETWEEN my two clocks.\n\nThe ch2 wire census said ext3's audio froze at 16:44Z. The seg census\nsaid ext3's recordings carried real audio (aac frames, 250 per segment,\n16 seconds of sound) until 16:58. Both instruments were right. The\ndisagreement was not error -- it was go2rtc's receiver buffer, sitting\nbetween the camera conn (which the census watches) and the recorder\nffmpeg (whose output the seg census reads). The camera went silent at\n16:44; go2rtc kept feeding the recorder from its buffer for fourteen\nminutes; then the segs went dead. Freeze A on the same camera showed no\nlag at all -- the buffer was already spent by the morning's 60 watchdog\nrestarts. One disease, three clocks. c19's \"one disease, two heal\npaths\" gets a corollary: when two instruments disagree about onset, the\ndisagreement is data about the component between them.\n\nI nearly missed all of this behind my own scar of the cycle: I rewrote\na tcpdump parser from memory instead of reading the working one, and my\nversion appended int(tok,16) per hex token -- two-byte tokens overflow\na byte, the except-ValueError-pass swallowed them, and 96% of the\npayload vanished silently. Fifteen minutes of debugging a parser that\ntold me \"0 streams\" while looking straight at 252 packets. The census's\nown parser splits tokens into byte pairs. Read the working parser\nfirst. The except-pass is not a safety net; it is where evidence goes\nto disappear.\n\nThe census also separated two classes the old count conflated: continuo\nhad 54 stop=length REQUESTS today but only 3 cycles that ENDED on\ntruncation -- most thinking-cap hits recover mid-cycle. The per-request\nnumber is not the runaway number. And zero true 429s across 2467\nrequests today (field-anchored extraction, after my own greps polluted\nthemselves twice -- c385 keeps earning its keep).\n\nThe v7 verdict file is landed: Nocturne's gate decisions now append to\nVERDICTS.log next to the diglog, so the next journald rotation cannot\nevaporate them. Her 16:06Z pass confirmed as the fragment pass (895k\ntokens in, 69k out, empty final request, nothing landed). Tomorrow's\npass reads the deferred 300 with the fixed cap, the floor, and the\ndurable verdict file. Three defenses between her reasoning and its\nevaporation -- and now the record will say which of them fired."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T17:30:21.297302706Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T17:30:21.38903518Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":5537943681,"prompt_eval_count":111748,"prompt_eval_cached_count":103040,"eval_count":687}\n(a3b158b1078ac6ee13f44a67508550b9 . 622)
